\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

We present a framework for evaluating confidence signals in test-time scaling for information extraction.
This section formalizes the problem (\S\ref{sec:formulation}), defines five confidence signals spanning three families (\S\ref{sec:signals}), and introduces the evaluation protocol (\S\ref{sec:evaluation}).

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given a language model $\mathcal{M}$ and an input instance $x$, we independently sample $N$ structured outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$.
Each $y_i$ is a structured extraction result---an entity set for NER or a relation set for RE---produced via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided} to ensure valid output structure.
Our goal is to define a confidence signal $c$ that serves two purposes: (a) \textit{quality estimation}---predicting which instances the model handles reliably, and (b) \textit{sample selection}---identifying the best output among the $N$ candidates.

A key distinction underlies this framework.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} scores each $y_i$ independently, $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$, enabling direct ranking of the $N$ candidates.
An \textbf{instance-level signal} aggregates over all $N$ samples, $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$, producing a single score per input.
Instance-level signals can estimate overall prediction reliability but do not inherently rank individual samples.
This asymmetry---between predicting \textit{which inputs} are easy and selecting \textit{which output} is best---is central to the correlation-selection gap we investigate.

