\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

We compare five signals from three families: \textit{model-internal} (log-probability), \textit{surface} (exact match, Fleiss' $\kappa$), and \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard, entity voting).

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
The only per-sample signal in our comparison.
For a sample $y_i$ consisting of tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$, we define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}
This is the length-normalized mean log-likelihood, reflecting the model's internal confidence in its generation.
Because it scores each sample independently, it can directly rank the $N$ candidates for sample selection.

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
Following self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency}, we measure pairwise agreement across samples at the string level:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{\binom{N}{2}} \sum_{i<j} \mathbb{1}[y_i = y_j]
\end{equation}
Equivalently, this can be expressed as the majority fraction $\max_y |\{i : y_i = y\}| / N$.
EM treats entire outputs as atomic units: two samples either match exactly or not, with no notion of partial overlap.

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt the inter-rater agreement measure of \citet{fleiss1971agreement} by treating the $N$ samples as independent annotators, token positions as items, and the token vocabulary as the set of categories:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is the observed agreement rate across token positions and $\bar{P}_e$ is the agreement expected by chance.
Unlike EM, FK captures partial agreement---two samples that share most tokens but differ in a few positions receive a high score.
However, FK operates at the string level and does not account for the structure of the extracted output.

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
Our core structural signal extends Jaccard similarity \cite{jaccard1901distribution} to operate on extracted entity or relation \textit{sets} rather than raw strings.
For two samples $y_i$ and $y_j$, we first group extractions by entity type.
Within each group, we compute a bipartite matching using the Hungarian algorithm with span overlap as the similarity metric.
The pairwise Soft Jaccard score is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{|M|}{\max(|A_i|, |A_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $|M|$ is the number of matched extraction pairs and $|A_i|$, $|A_j|$ are the extraction counts of each sample.
The instance-level signal averages over all sample pairs:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj_instance}
c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \frac{1}{\binom{N}{2}} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)
\end{equation}
Two design choices distinguish SJ from surface signals.
First, type-aware grouping prevents spurious matches between extractions of different entity types.
Second, span-overlap matching tolerates near-synonym variations (e.g., ``machine learning'' vs.\ ``ML''), which would be penalized by exact string comparison.

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
While SJ compares entire extraction sets, entity voting operates at the granularity of individual extractions.
For each entity $e$ appearing in the union $E_\cup$ of all $N$ samples, we compute its frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:freq}
\text{freq}(e) = \frac{|\{i : e \in y_i\}|}{N}
\end{equation}
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency across all observed entities:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
This adapts majority voting \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} from discrete answer selection to set-valued extraction.
Entities that appear consistently across samples contribute high frequency; hallucinated or idiosyncratic extractions that appear in only one or two samples pull the score down.
This per-entity granularity enables the signal to distinguish instances where the model reliably extracts a core set of entities (high $c_{\text{vote}}$) from those where extractions vary widely across samples (low $c_{\text{vote}}$).

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

We assess confidence signals along two complementary dimensions.

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Quality estimation asks: \textit{for which instances does the model produce reliable outputs?}
We measure rank correlation between the confidence signal and instance-level F1 via Spearman $\rho$ and Kendall $\tau$.
For binary discrimination, we compute AUROC by classifying instances as above or below median F1.
These metrics evaluate whether a signal can identify reliable vs.\ unreliable predictions without access to gold labels.

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Sample selection asks: \textit{given $N$ candidates for a single instance, which one is best?}
We report best-of-$N$ F1: for each instance, the signal selects one sample, and we compute micro F1 over all instances.
We compare against an \textit{oracle} (which always selects the highest-F1 sample) and a \textit{greedy baseline} ($T{=}0$, $N{=}1$).
Per-sample signals like log-probability directly rank the $N$ candidates.
Instance-level signals (SJ, FK, EM, Voting) produce a single score per instance and thus require an auxiliary strategy for sample selection---we select the sample closest to the consensus output, i.e., the one with highest average pairwise similarity to all other samples.

\paragraph{The two dimensions are complementary, not redundant.}
A signal with high rank correlation accurately predicts relative instance difficulty, making it useful for quality-aware routing or selective prediction.
However, high correlation does not guarantee effective sample selection: if per-sample variance is low---the model produces $N$ similar outputs for a given input---even a perfect quality estimator cannot distinguish among near-identical candidates.
We investigate this decoupling empirically in \S\ref{sec:selection_gap}.
